module ui.sdl3
//! `ui.backend`'s two traits over SDL3: [SdlPlatform], a window and its events, and
//! [SdlRenderer], which draws a display list as triangles through SDL's 2D renderer
//! (`SDL_RenderGeometry`).
//!
//! ```
//! import ui.sdl3
//!
//! let Ok(window) = sdl3.open("Hello", 640.0, 480.0) else { return 1 }
//! var platform = window.platform
//! var renderer = window.renderer
//! // … platform.next_events(…), renderer.draw(…) …
//! sdl3.close(platform, renderer)
//! ```
//!
//! **Shapes are triangles** (`ui.tess`), tessellated in pixels after the display list is
//! scaled, so a rounded corner is as smooth on a Retina display as anywhere. A run of fills
//! and borders is one draw; a texture or a clip ends the run.
//!
//! **SDL's software renderer puts a vertex on a whole pixel** (it truncates), which a GPU
//! renderer does not: there, the half-pixel band that softens an edge lands half a pixel out
//! and a hairline shows as two pixels at half strength. It is the renderer a machine with no
//! GPU — and a headless test — gets.
//!
//! **A clip is a rectangle**: SDL's renderer clips to whole pixels and nothing rounder, so a
//! `PushClip`'s radii are ignored here — content in a rounded clip shows square at its
//! corners. A backend that can mask (a GPU's stencil) honours them.
//!
//! **Logical units**: SDL reports a window in points, and a high-density display shows a
//! point as several pixels. A logical unit is a point times the pixel density over the
//! display scale ([SdlPlatform.scale]) — the same as a point on a Mac, smaller than one on
//! Windows at 150%, where the user asked for everything larger.

